#X text 50 172 SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) is a synchronous serial
interface designed for high-speed data exchange between a microcontroller
(the master device) and peripheral devices (the slave devices)., f 79;
#X obj 40 172 cnv 1 2 55 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 50 232 The range of SPI applications on the Raspberry Pi 4/5
is extremely wide. Thanks to its high speed \, the interface is used
for connecting TFT displays \, where fast frame rendering is required
\, as well as for high-precision ADCs (analog-to-digital converters)
\, since the Pi itself has no analog inputs. In industrial prototyping
\, SPI is valued for the determinism and reliability of the connection., f 79;
#X obj 40 232 cnv 1 2 101 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 50 338 On the RPi4 and RPi5 \, the number of SPI buses reaches
seven (from SPI0 to SPI6) \, however by default they are disabled.
The use of certain buses may conflict with other functions (HDMI \,
cameras), f 79;
#X text 50 398 Standard SPI connection requires four lines: SCLK (clock
line) \, MOSI (master out \, slave in) \, MISO (master in \, slave
out) \, and CS (chip select) \, which the Master uses to activate a
specific device, f 79;
#X obj 20 458 cnv 5 400 28 empty empty Usage\ information 4 14 0 16
-70738 -262144 0;
#X text 50 506 The main difference between the available buses lies
in their implementation and performance. The SPI0 bus is a "full-featured"
one and supports all standard operating modes (Mode 0-3) \, has hardware
FIFO buffers and DMA support. It is ideal for high-load tasks., f 79;
#X text 50 581 The remaining buses (SPI1 \, SPI2 \, as well as SPI3-6)
are considered "auxiliary" (mini SPI). They have a smaller buffer \,
do not support DMA \, and may require more processor time at high frequencies.
In addition \, auxiliary buses may not support some modes \, for example
\, Mode 1 or Mode 3\., f 79;
#X text 50 656 When choosing a bus for a project \, it is important
to consider this: any bus will work for simple sensors \, but for a
high-speed display or data collection \, it is better to use SPI0\., f 79;
#X obj 20 716 cnv 5 400 28 empty empty Configuration 4 14 0 16 -70738
-262144 0;
#X text 50 764 Setting up SPI on the Raspberry Pi starts with its activation
\, as the interface is disabled by default. The standard method is
using the raspi-config utility. In the terminal \, it is necessary
to run sudo raspi-config \, then go to "Interface Options" and enable
SPI. After rebooting \, device files /dev/spidev0.0 and /dev/spidev0.1
will appear in the system., f 79;
#X text 50 854 To enable additional buses (for example \, SPI1 or SPI3
on the RPi5) \, manual editing of the /boot/firmware/config.txt file
is required. By adding a line like dtoverlay=spi1-3cs (where 3cs means
the number of Chip Select lines) \, the user activates the desired
interface with the required number of slots for connecting devices., f 79;
#X obj 1 964 cnv 5 680 40 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -203890 -1 0;